 "notes": "Things to check:\n(1) Portland: the station list has only 'portland-bill', but the 1886–87 observations name 'Portland (Low)'. I mapped all of them to portland-bill. If you want High and Low kept apart, add a separate Low entry.\n(2) St Ann's High in 1886 (n=1191): mapped to the single-row st-anns-head. Use st-anns-head-high instead if you want the High light kept distinct.\n(3) Bare town names that may stand for a light: Holyhead (n=594, mapped to holyhead-breakwater; it could be South Stack), Falmouth (n=378, made a place; it could be st-anthony-head), Portland (n=1007, mapped to portland-bill).\n(4) Island names with listed lights made stations: Caldy Island (n=198) and St Tudwal's Island (n=1192). Calf of Man (n=203) is made a place, not Chicken Rock.\n(5) Type words that contradict the list: Wolf Rock 'L.V.' (n=1418) and Godrevy 'L.v.' (n=485). Both are lighthouses and are mapped to them.\n(6) Allonby is a station only in 1881. The 1886–87 Allonby records (n=47, 55) are made places.\n(7) Unidentified: 'Baytor Mere, Devon' (n=116; perhaps Haytor). 'Grimsdale' (n=515) is taken as Grinsdale, Cumberland. 'Gretna' (n=511) is filed under Cumberland but is in Dumfriesshire.\n(8) Unresolved by design: 'S.W. stations' (n=1064) is an unnamed group, and 'this station' (n=1523) needs each observation's context.\n(9) Garbles read as: Felwick/Hlelwick/flelwick = Helwick; Fiver/fiwwer Dee = River Dee; Mena = Menai (trwyn-du); Laneness = Langness; Nash (#.)/(£.)/(H.) = Nash (E.) high light, the only Nash listed in 1886.",
